# Badges A–B — checked against official scouting.org excerpts.

@owner mb.athletics
@note The 2026 update removed the physical-exam requirement and added career options; excerpts mixed old and new text, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Athletics builds an all-around athletic program: safety and conditioning, measuring your performance in running, jumping, throwing and other events, training to improve, and exploring careers or lifelong fitness through athletics.
@safety Warm up, train progressively and follow your coach’s and counselor’s safety guidance.

@owner mb.automotive-maintenance
@complete partial
@note Numbers follow the order in which the official page lists the topic areas; sub-items, which include electric and hybrid vehicles, are summarized. You need access to a vehicle and its owner’s manual.
@desc Automotive Maintenance teaches how a car or truck works and how to care for it — safety, dashboards, tires, engine, cooling, fuel, ignition and electrical systems, drive train and brakes, including electric and hybrid vehicles.
@safety Work on vehicles only with a knowledgeable adult, on a level surface with the engine off and cool, and never under a vehicle supported only by a jack.
1 k! Safety and ownership :: Safety around vehicles, registration and the costs of owning a vehicle.
2 a General maintenance :: Routine maintenance schedules and checks.
3 k Dashboard and indicators :: Dashboard gauges and warning indicators.
4 demo Tires :: Tire care, pressure and changing a tire.
5 k Engine :: How the engine works and engine maintenance.
6 k Cooling system :: The cooling system and its care.
7 k Fuel system :: The fuel system and its care.
8 k Ignition and electrical :: The ignition and electrical systems.
9 k Drive train :: The drive train and transmission.
10 k Brakes :: The braking system and its care.
11 a Evaluating and caring for vehicles :: Options such as evaluating vehicles and cleaning or waxing a car.

@owner mb.aviation
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/skills/merit-badges/digital-resource-guides/aviation/ | Aviation Digital Resource Guide (scouting.org)
@desc Aviation covers how aircraft fly, building and flying a model or drone, flight operations such as preflight inspections and simulators, visiting airports and aviation facilities, and the paths to flying and aviation careers.
@safety Drone flights must follow FAA rules; earn the required recreational drone certification before flying.
1 k Aviation basics :: Kinds of aircraft, the history of flight, engines, the four forces, lift, control surfaces and cockpit instruments.
2 a Principles of flight :: One option: a model glider, a rubber-band airplane, or a powered model or drone with the required certification.
3 a Flight operations :: Two options such as flight-simulator practice, a preflight inspection, observing maintenance, reading charts or a discovery flight.
4 a Airport operations :: One option: visiting an airport, an FAA facility, a military aviation facility, or an aviation museum or air show.
5 k Aviation opportunities :: Pilot certificates and ratings, aviation youth programs and aviation careers.

@owner mb.backpacking
@complete complete
@desc Backpacking prepares you to travel self-sufficiently on multi-day treks: trail health, lightweight gear, crew organization and safety, Leave No Trace, water, navigation, weather, stoves and meals, packing, and a series of treks building to a five-day trip.
@safety Treks need an approved plan, an emergency response plan and appropriate adult leadership.
@study study.camp-ready
1 k! Trail health :: Preventing and treating health problems on the trail, such as heat and cold reactions and blisters.
= mb.camping.1c
2 k Gear and weight :: The Scout Basic Essentials and ways to reduce pack weight and bulk.
3 k! Crew organization and safety :: Crew size, organizing a crew, reducing risk and emergency response plans.
4 k Leave No Trace :: Leave No Trace and the Outdoor Code on treks, and handling waste and personal cleanliness.
= mb.camping.2
5 demo! Water :: Treating water by two methods and staying hydrated.
= mb.camping.6b
6 demo Navigation :: Reading a topographic map and finding your position with a map and compass or GPS.
7 k! Weather :: Preparing for and handling bad weather on a trek.
8 m! Stoves and meals :: Stove types and fuels, safe stove use, cooking trail meals and storing food.
9 a Planning and packing :: A short hike schedule, inspecting patrol gear, packing and fitting your pack, and a loaded practice hike.
10 a Treks :: Several multi-day treks of the required length.
11 a An extended trek :: Planning and completing a longer trek with a budget and a service project, and keeping a journal.

@owner mb.basketry
@note Excerpts showed the topics (tool and material safety, basket types and weaves, and three projects) but not their numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Basketry is a hands-on craft badge: safe use of tools and materials, the main kinds of baskets and weaves, and weaving several projects such as a square basket, a round basket and a seat.

@owner mb.bird-study
@complete partial
@source https://www.scouting.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Advancement-updates-2026_2.pdf | Advancement Updates 2026 (scouting.org)
@note The 2026 update added a Leave No Trace requirement and career options. Excerpts combined some topics; requirements 6 and 7 are separated here by topic and should be confirmed.
@desc Bird Study teaches you to observe and identify birds by sight and sound, understand their adaptations and role in ecosystems, help them through habitat projects, and practice outdoor ethics while birding.
@study study.camp-ready
1 k Birds and ecosystems :: Why birds are useful environmental indicators and how they fit into ecosystems.
2 k Bird anatomy :: The parts of a perched bird and its wing feathers.
3 k Optics :: Choosing and caring for binoculars or a spotting scope.
4 k Field guides :: Using a field guide and its range maps.
5 a Identifying birds :: Identifying wild bird species and keeping a field notebook.
6 k Adaptations :: How birds’ bodies and plumage suit their way of life.
7 a Songs and calls :: Bird songs and calls and identifying birds by sound.
8 a Bird surveys :: One option: a field trip, a bird count or a banding program.
9 a A backyard project :: One project such as a feeder, birdbath, nest box or habitat plan.
10 k Protecting birds :: Endangered and threatened species and habitat protection.
11 k Non-native birds :: Non-native birds and their effects on ecosystems.
12 d Ethics in birding :: Leave No Trace and the Outdoor Code as they apply to bird study.
13 a Careers or hobbies :: One option: careers related to birds, or a lifelong hobby.
